---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro";
import CopyCommand from "../../components/CopyCommand.tsx";
import { APP_STORE_URL, IPHONE_APP_LIVE } from "../../lib/links";

// Every command here was run against the repo's test host (npm run testhost:up)
// on 2026-10-03. What it says about other tools is from their own docs on that
// date; recheck them, and bump `updated` in lib/guides.ts, when this changes.
const SCP = `scp ~/Desktop/shot.png myserver:/tmp/shot.png`;
const SHOT = `shot() {
  local name="shot-$(date +%s).png"
  pngpaste "/tmp/$name" &&
    scp -q "/tmp/$name" "\${1:-myserver}:/tmp/$name" &&
    printf '/tmp/%s' "$name" | pbcopy
}`;
---

<Guide slug="paste-a-screenshot-into-claude-code-over-ssh">
  <p class="lede">
    When Claude Code runs on a server you reach over SSH, Ctrl+V doesn't paste your screenshot.
    Every fix comes down to the same two steps: put the image file on the server, then give the
    agent its path.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Why Ctrl+V doesn't work</h2>
    <p>
      Claude Code reads a pasted image from the clipboard of the machine it runs on. Over SSH,
      that's the server. Your screenshot is on your Mac's clipboard, and your terminal only sends
      the server text, so the image never arrives. Anthropic closed the request to make it work over
      SSH as not planned (<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/42712">#42712</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What every fix relies on: a path becomes the picture</h2>
    <p>
      Paste the path of an image file on the server, such as
      <code class="mono">/tmp/shot.png</code>, and Claude Code attaches the image itself, exactly
      as if you had pasted the picture. Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode do the same. So the whole job
      is getting the file onto the server and its path into the prompt.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. By hand, with scp</h2>
    <p>
      Fine when it happens once a day. Press <code class="mono">⌘⇧4</code> to save a screenshot to
      the Desktop, rename it <code class="mono">shot.png</code> (macOS names it with spaces), then:
    </p>
    <div class="cmd"><CopyCommand command={SCP} client:visible /></div>
    <p>
      Paste <code class="mono">/tmp/shot.png</code> into Claude Code. Replace
      <code class="mono">myserver</code> with the host you SSH into.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. A shell function</h2>
    <p>
      To skip the file on the Desktop, copy the screenshot instead (<code class="mono">⌃⌘⇧4</code>)
      and let <a href="https://github.com/jcsalterego/pngpaste">pngpaste</a> write it out
      (<code class="mono">brew install pngpaste</code>). Put this in your
      <code class="mono">~/.zshrc</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="term">{SHOT}</pre>
    <p>
      Run <code class="mono">shot</code>, or <code class="mono">shot otherhost</code>, and your
      clipboard holds the path, ready to paste. If there's no image on the clipboard or the upload
      fails, it stops and leaves your clipboard alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      It covers the everyday case. It doesn't send several screenshots at once or a file from
      Finder, show you which host you're about to send to, or work from your phone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Let your terminal do it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b><a rel="nofollow" href="https://cmux.com/docs/ssh">cmux</a></b>, a free, open-source Mac terminal for
        coding agents: drop an image or a file into a remote session and it uploads it with scp over
        the session's own connection.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b><a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/kittens/clipboard/">kitty</a></b>: its clipboard
        kitten works over SSH, so <code class="mono">kitten clipboard -g shot.png</code> run on the
        server saves your clipboard image there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b><a rel="nofollow" href="https://docs.termius.com/terminal/mobile-terminal">Termius</a></b> on iPhone
        and Android uploads an image you paste into a session.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>These work inside that one app, so you're tied to it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Keep the agent's window on your Mac</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop">Claude Desktop</a></b> can run Claude
        Code on a remote machine over SSH, with the app as the interface. You attach or drop a
        screenshot into its prompt as you would locally.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/remote-control">Remote Control</a></b> lets you
        follow a Claude Code session from the Claude app or claude.ai/code and attach photos there.
        It needs a Pro, Max, Team or Enterprise sign-in, and isn't available with an API key, on
        Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud or Microsoft Foundry, through an LLM gateway, or in Zero Data
        Retention organizations.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both are Claude only.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Hostbeam</h2>
    <p>
      Copy a screenshot, press <code class="mono">⌘⇧7</code>, and Hostbeam beams it to the host you
      picked, over your own SSH, and copies its path. Paste it into any agent, in any terminal.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Any file too: copy it in Finder and press the shortcut, or drop it on the menu-bar icon.</li>
      <li>Several screenshots at once arrive as several pictures in Claude Code.</li>
      <li>
        The host key is checked before anything is sent, a name never overwrites a file, and a
        failed beam leaves your clipboard as it was.
      </li>
      {
        IPHONE_APP_LIVE ? (
          <li>
            From the iPhone, share any screenshot to Hostbeam (<a href={APP_STORE_URL}>App Store</a>).
          </li>
        ) : (
          <li>From the iPhone, through the share sheet, once the app is on the App Store.</li>
        )
      }
      <li>
        With the free <a href="/agents/">MCP server</a> on the host, the agent fetches your latest
        beam itself, with nothing to paste.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which one to use</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>If you</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>send a screenshot now and then</td><td>scp by hand</td></tr>
          <tr><td>want it free and don't mind a script</td><td>the shell function</td></tr>
          <tr><td>already live in cmux or kitty</td><td>your terminal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>use only Claude, signed in with a plan</td><td>Claude Desktop or Remote Control</td></tr>
          <tr><td>want one shortcut for any agent and any terminal</td><td>Hostbeam</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      More apps do this than fit here:
      <a href="/guides/mac-tools-that-send-screenshots-to-a-remote-agent/">every Mac tool for it,
      compared</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
